A||N||6||varnster||restoring hard plastic||||1||
Z||000000||varnster||12-11-2000||08:53 PM||||Just wondering whats the best way to get the<BR>hard plastic items looking new again. I.E<BR>fan shrouding,head light plastic, that kind of stuff, mine is in good condition[mechanicly] just a bit weathered. allso tips on the interior plastic. thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||Old Lar||12-11-2000||09:03 PM||||I use Mothers Back to Black.  Its a cleaner that will make black plastic, like the black cowle in front of the windshield or plastic stuff under the hood, windshield rubber gasket, weather stripping etc.  It is only a temporary fix..looks good for a couple of days.<P>The only real fix is clean it real good and paint with a high gloss plastic paint.||||reg||3
Z||000002||theogre||12-11-2000||09:08 PM||||Try cleaning fan srouding and that sort of thing with "basin tub and tile" cleaner and the really soft Scotch Brite pads. The soft pads won't leave such obvious scratches. This will get off all the gunk plus take off the top oxidized plastic. Warning: this stuff can also erase text from labels if it gets on them.<P>Total Shine will remove wax and junk from things you can't scrub with Scotch Brite but the finish it leaves is only temporary. This also helps the looks of the mirrors and if you have it the lugage rack. Anything with that flatish paint finish.||||reg||1
Z||000003||varnster||12-11-2000||09:24 PM||||would I need to use a high temp paint under<BR>the hood around the coolant system, any particular brand that holds up better?||||reg||1
Z||000004||horseman||12-11-2000||09:30 PM||||I have always had good success with those flat back trim items by repainting them. It makes them appear as new. Try sccuffing them up with 240 grit and then 400 grit. Tack cloth them,or alcohol and a lint free rag. Then lightly coat them with rustoleum flat black. Two or three light coats will suffice and last for at least a year.<P>Charlie ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tom88gt||12-11-2000||09:34 PM||||I use silicone spray on everything plastic, rubber or vinyl.  I've had real good results.  If your plastic is old and dry, you can see it just suck up that silicone.  Won't completely restore it but will return some of the flexibility and "give" to the plastic.||||reg||3
Z||000006||FieroJoe||12-11-2000||10:45 PM||||Try some Westley's Bleche Wite on the stuff you want, and then either armor all (lasts about 3 days) or Meguiar's Endurance Wheel Gel (lasts about 15 days) on it.<BR>    I've repainted all of my round molding after sanding it to 600 grit, with ACE premium enamel, gloss black.  Came out great.  Also did the decklid vents, and they look awesome!<P>------------------<BR>FieroJoe<BR>c311t@yahoo.com<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
